<p>Myanmar’s military-led government announced a New Year amnesty for 4,335 prisoners on April 17, freeing former president Win Myint and cutting Aung San Suu Kyi’s prison term by one-sixth. Suu Kyi, 80, had been serving a 27-year sentence on charges including incitement, corruption, election fraud, and violating the state secrets law. It remains unclear whether the reduced term could lead to house arrest. The move came during Myanmar’s traditional New Year period, in a country still shaped by the military’s 2021 coup, which removed Suu Kyi’s elected government.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/HkvZs_Ab1vmedkcD1c-14JDjMyzWvP3yOgST4m3LNFeo_RZdw0VGiINudghn-jDynpGZfskIQxK5Zu3Kln9Ye7yN6oAziES45QBCIlc7Yt1o' alt='File photo of Myanmar’s ousted civilian leader Aung San Suu Kyi.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Conditional release: State broadcaster MRTV said authorities freed Win Myint under section 401(1), a form of conditional pardon whose exact terms were not made public.</li><li>Public absence: Suu Kyi has not appeared in public since her lengthy trials ended, and her whereabouts have not been publicly disclosed.</li><li>Prison scene: Relatives gathered outside Yangon’s Insein Prison as prisoners walked free under the amnesty, a vivid sign of how quickly the announcement affected families of detainees.</li><li>Approval chain: State media said President Min Aung Hlaing approved the amnesty.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Myanmar military-backed government: State media cast the announcement as a formal clemency measure, with Win Myint’s release folded into a broader official amnesty. (<a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/myanmar-free-more-than-4000-prisoners-amnesty-2026-04-17/'>Reuters</a>)</li><li>Aung San Suu Kyi’s allies and legal side: Her allies say the charges used to imprison her were politically motivated and meant to keep her out of politics. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992670/myanmar-cuts-ex-leader-aung-san-suu-kyis-sentence-frees-former-president'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>United Nations: UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres responded cautiously, saying the move should be followed by the swift release of all those arbitrarily detained, including Suu Kyi. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992670/myanmar-cuts-ex-leader-aung-san-suu-kyis-sentence-frees-former-president'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992670/myanmar-cuts-ex-leader-aung-san-suu-kyis-sentence-frees-former-president'>Myanmar cuts ex-leader Aung San Suu Kyi’s sentence, frees former president</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/myanmars-suu-kyi-sentence-reduced-under-mass-amnesty-source/article70873420.ece'>Myanmar ex-President freed from post-coup detention, Suu Kyi’s sentence cut</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/former-president-win-myint-freed-in-broad-myanmar-prisoner-amnesty'>Former President Win Myint freed in broad Myanmar prisoner amnesty</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/myanmar-free-more-than-4000-prisoners-amnesty-2026-04-17/'>Myanmar&#x27;s ousted president Win Myint is released under an amnesty on Myanmar’s New Year’s Day.</a> - reuters.com</li></ul>